# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The tracked tree is exactly the deliverable layout from the assignment spec:
#
#   task1/src/*.cpp, task1/task1_report.pdf
#   task2/src/*.cpp, task2/task2_report.pdf
#   plots/*.png
#   README.md
#
# plus the starter scaffolding needed to actually build and grade the code
# (Makefile, include/, main.cpp, the naive baselines, task READMEs, the
# llama.cpp injection scripts). Everything else below is ignored.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Build output (bin/ matches at any depth: task1/bin/, task2/bin/)
bin/
solution/
*.o
*.d
*.dSYM/
.DS_Store

# Local benchmarking harnesses (drivers, sweep scripts, width variants).
# Not part of the deliverable; used only to produce the numbers in the reports.
task1/bench/
task2/bench/

# Raw measurement data (CSV/TXT/log sweeps). The reports and plots/ carry the
# results that matter; the raw dumps stay local.
task1/results/
task2/results/

# LaTeX sources and build artifacts for the reports. Only the compiled
# task1/task1_report.pdf and task2/task2_report.pdf are tracked.
task1/report/
task2/report/
*.aux
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz
*.toc

# Assignment handout (course-provided, ~5 MB, not our work)
CS683 PA1 2026.pdf

# Task 2 llama.cpp demo (`make llama-demo`): full checkout + built binaries +
# downloaded model weights. All regenerable, all large.
task2/llama/llama.cpp/
task2/llama/models/
*.gguf

# Python/venv scratch used for plotting
__pycache__/
.venv/
venv/
